{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786993884,"nonce":"MWhgO5fItsRiNQ6ZzwuxryAHnrw=","title":"The Slack thread's identity is derived from a grouping oto does not control and cannot reproduce","message":"**What is wrong.** `ComputeGroupKey(orgID, sourceID, receiver, groupLabels)`\n(`internal/alerts/domain/keys.go:167`) derives oto's group — and under ADR 0005 the identity of\na Slack thread — from Alertmanager's `group_by`. oto neither chooses that grouping nor can\nrecompute it, so the operator's config file silently decides how many threads oto posts.\n\n**The demonstration.** Three consequences, each observable today.\n\n*Matchers can only see group labels.* There is exactly one policy match entry —\n`internal/notification/service/notify.go:269` → `policy.go:93` — and it is fed\n`snap.Group.GroupLabels`; the reminder path uses `g.GroupLabels` (`reminder.go:127`). That is\ncorrect rather than lazy, because group labels are the only label set true of *every* member.\nBut it means a policy matching `namespace` matches nothing unless the operator put `namespace`\nin `group_by`, and it fails quietly, as a `no_policy` suppression rather than an error.\n\n*The reconciler cannot build a group at all.* `GET /api/v2/alerts` returns no grouping, so\nreconciler-sourced groups get an empty receiver and no group labels —\n`db/migrations/00008_grouping.sql:81` records exactly that, citing SPEC §C.4. Two ingest paths,\ntwo different answers to \"which thread does this belong to\".\n\n*`continue: true` yields two threads for one alert.* `receiver` is in the key, and\n`internal/sources/client/alertmanager/amroutes.go:31` names continue as \"where multi-delivery\ncomes from\", so one alert reaching two receivers occupies two groups at once — which\n`alert_group_members`' `PRIMARY KEY (group_id, occurrence_id)` permits by design. That\nmany-to-many shape is itself the proof that group membership is not an identity claim: a thing\ncannot be identical to two different things.\n\nEditing `alertmanager.yml` shifts the whole key space — new groups, new threads, the in-flight\nones orphaned.\n\n**Why it matters.** An Alertmanager group is a declared notification-batching boundary, not a\nstatement that its members are related. Hanging thread identity on it puts oto's most visible\noutput under the control of a file oto can neither read back nor validate, per receiver.\nMeanwhile the labels oto has already promoted as meaningful are on every alert on both ingest\npaths: `alertname`, `severity`, `namespace`, `service` at\n`internal/alerts/domain/labels.go:37-49`, plus `cluster`, which\n`internal/grouping/domain/group.go:169` already makes first-class \"rather than read out of\ngroupLabels\".\n\n**Where.** `internal/alerts/domain/keys.go:167`;\n`internal/grouping/domain/group.go:169-215`; `internal/grouping/service/service.go:220`;\n`internal/notification/service/notify.go:269`; `internal/sources/service/reconcile.go:529`;\n`db/migrations/00008_grouping.sql:15-89`; `docs/adr/0005-durable-group-key-owns-the-slack-thread.md`.\n\n**Scope.** large\n\n**Done when.** The group key is `f(org, cluster, alertname, namespace-or-∅)` — computed from\nthe alert's own labels, identically on the ingest and reconciler paths, and **fixed rather than\nconfigurable**. A tunable split key reinvents `group_by` inside oto and re-inherits the problem;\nSPEC's `correlation` charter already words the requirement as \"machine-derived groupings… with\na **stated** algorithm\".\n\n`severity` is deliberately not an axis — an escalation is the same problem getting worse, and\ngroup severity is an aggregate that only means something if both live in one group. Nor is\n`pod`/`instance`, which is the thing being grouped. `service` is omitted until evidence says\notherwise. An absent `namespace` is its own partition, not an error.\n\nADR 0005 needs no amendment: the group still owns exactly one thread. `alert_groups.receiver`\nand `source_group_key` become vestigial and are dropped — `ingest_batches` already records both\nalongside the raw payload, `notification_reason` and `truncated_alerts`. An ADR records that\ndropping `receiver` from the key merges routes that deliberately separated the same alerts, and\nthat `cluster_key` is what must distinguish them.\n\nValidate the axes before committing to them. The key is computable from\n`ingest_batches.payload`, retained 30 days: replay a week and compare the thread count the rule\nwould have produced against what Alertmanager's grouping actually produced.","files":null}]}